---
// Lesson 11.4b — MC Practice 2: Data & Networks
// assessment: targeted set 2 — DAT/AAP-1 + CSN families; error table v2.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderMcqSet } from '../../lib/mc-quiz';

const KEYS = '../data/mcq-keys.json';

const body = `<h1>11.4b — MC Practice 2: Data &amp; Networks</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Target practice. Set 2 drills the data families (DAT-1, DAT-2,
    AAP-1 — binary, lists, filtering) and the network families
    (CSN-1, CSN-2 — packets, routing, redundancy). These are the
    questions your <a href="9.1.html">9.1</a>–<a href="9.9.html">9.9</a>
    units mapped directly, so today is verification: do you score
    what you learned?
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity assessment">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Warm Drill</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>Round the bases, 90 seconds each — write answers, then
    check neighbors:</p>
    <ol>
        <li>(DAT-1) A list of 32 student names — minimum bits to
            give each a unique ID?</li>
        <li>(DAT-2) The dataset column "rating 1–5" — which
            operation answers "most common rating", and how would
            you loop it in pseudocode?</li>
        <li>(CSN-1) Why can the internet "route around damage"?</li>
        <li>(CSN-2) Why is UDP right for game packets and TCP
            right for the game's download? (<a href="9.8.html">9.8</a>)</li>
    </ol>

    <details class="wt-final wt-gated">
        <summary>
            <span class="wt-gated-title">Check your answers</span>
            <span class="wt-gated-peek">write yours, then compare with your neighbor</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="wt-lead">Then compare. Every one of these is a family you met earlier this year — the check is whether the words come back with the lesson closed.</p>
        <ol>
            <li>(DAT-1) 2⁵ = 32, so <strong>5 bits</strong> — five bits to name 32 students, not thirty-two bits.</li>
            <li>(DAT-2) The <strong>mode</strong>: a tally per value, then take the largest. One counter per rating, one loop over the rows to fill them, one pass over the counters to find the biggest.</li>
            <li>(CSN-1) <strong>No single path:</strong> packets re-route, because a router decides locally instead of reading a map of the whole network.</li>
            <li>(CSN-2) <strong>Drop vs. wait</strong> (<a href="9.8.html">9.8</a>): UDP drops a late packet and keeps the game moving; TCP holds the download until every packet arrives, because half a download is worth nothing.</li>
        </ol>
    </details>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
${renderMcqSet(2, KEYS)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity assessment">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Error Table v2 &amp; Pair-Teach</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Grade the set, then work the table:</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li>Grade → error table v2 (family / time / right-wrong),
            cumulative with Set 1</li>
        <li>Compare to Set 1: did the weak family improve? If not
            — WHY not (misconception, not speed: wrong tells you
            what to re-read)</li>
        <li>The misconception hunt: for each wrong answer, write
            the sentence of what you BELIEVED. "The mode is the
            average" is a fixable sentence; "I guessed C" is
            not.</li>
    </ol>

    <p><strong>Pair-teach (10 min):</strong> find someone whose
    error table is strong where yours is weak. They explain their
    attack on 2 questions; you explain yours. Teaching is the
    fastest known study method — this is
    <a href="1.1.html">1.1</a>'s pair setup running one last
    time.</p>


    <div class="socratic">


        <h3>Socratic: Tagging the Misses</h3>


        <ol>


            <li>You tagged your misses by CED family. Which family did you think you knew, and what does the tag say?</li>


            <li>Bandwidth and latency are the classic trap pair. In your own words, which one does a bigger file hurt, and which one does distance hurt?</li>


            <li>You paired up to teach an error back. Which explanation changed your own answer — explaining it, or being explained to?</li>


        </ol>


    </div>



    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Score Set 2 with a family-labeled error table</li>
            <li>Write the misconception sentence for every miss</li>
            <li>Teach one question to someone whose table is weak there</li>
            <li>Know which families you'll drill before <a href="11.4c.html">11.4c</a></li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-09/binary-and-hex.html">Binary &amp; Hex</a> — DAT-1 family</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-09/the-internet.html">The Internet</a> — CSN-1/CSN-2 family</li>
        <li><a href="../exam/mc-strategy.html">MC Strategy</a> — three-pass reminder</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="11.4c.html">11.4c — MC Practice 3 + Written-Response Drill</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="11.4b" title="11.4b MC Practice 2: Data &amp; Networks — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
